/*********************************************************************
Configuration and initialization file of the MS-SQL <Project>
database. The database holds structured information that is logically
grouped by the domain of usage, one schema per domain. The file builds
the database from scratch, and a pipeline can run it again to bring
an existing database to the latest version.

All the DDL statements are guarded, i.e., the initialization scripts
should not fail if an object exists: schemas and tables check
SCHEMA_ID or OBJECT_ID first, and the views, functions, procedures
and triggers use CREATE OR ALTER (SQL Server 2016 SP1 and later).

The script runs in sqlcmd mode from this directory, against an
existing database, and stops at the first error. The whole run is one
transaction, so a failure rolls the database back:

    sqlcmd -S <server> -d <database> -b -i initialize.sql

SQL Server Replication is not configured here: run the scripts of
``replications/`` (one per publication) separately, after this file.

CREATE DATABASE cannot run inside a transaction, so create the
database once before the first run, for example with a UTF-8 collation
(2019 and later) and row versioning for readers (set while no other
connection is open):

    CREATE DATABASE <database>
        COLLATE Latin1_General_100_CI_AS_SC_UTF8;

    ALTER DATABASE <database> SET READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT ON;

Copyright © [<year>] <copyright-holder>
*********************************************************************/

:on error exit

SET NOCOUNT ON;
SET XACT_ABORT ON;

PRINT CONCAT(
    'Starting <Project> Initialization '
    , FORMAT(SYSDATETIMEOFFSET(), 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.fff zzz')
);
GO

/*********************************************************************
Schemas by Domain of Usage: the DDL of every schema lives in its own
directory under ``schemas/`` with its own ``initialize.sql``, and is
loaded in a strict order, as the later schemas reference the earlier
ones:

    1. ``core``     : shared master data and helper functions that
        every other schema references; always initialized first.
    2. ``auth``     : identity - users, clients, roles and sessions.
    3. ``events``   : what happened in the system - transactions,
        bills, jobs and logs; references ``core`` and ``auth``.
    4. ``projects`` : objects of a single project or product.

NOTE: Never create application objects in the ``dbo`` schema on a
production server. A one-part name falls back to ``dbo``, and every
object in it shares one owner, so an ownership chain can reach all of
them; keep ``dbo`` empty and use the named schemas above.
*********************************************************************/

BEGIN TRANSACTION;
GO

PRINT '  >> Creating Core Schema ...';

IF SCHEMA_ID(N'core') IS NULL
    EXEC (N'CREATE SCHEMA core AUTHORIZATION <owner-role>;');
GO

PRINT '  >> Creating Core Table(s) (optionally seed) ...';
:r schemas/core/initialize.sql

PRINT '  >> Creating other Schemas and Optionally Seed Table(s) ...';
:r schemas/auth/initialize.sql
:r schemas/events/initialize.sql
:r schemas/projects/initialize.sql

COMMIT TRANSACTION;
GO

PRINT 'Completed <Project> Initialization';
GO
